---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import TimingComparison from "../../components/TimingComparison.astro";
import { macTimings } from "../../components/comparison-data";
---
<Base
  title="Two Rust photo apps on the Mac — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="The same test as last time, on the desktop. On the Mac, LightPhotos lets Apple decode the photos, and LightCraft decodes every photo with its own Rust code. That changes which app is faster, and where."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-10-07">October 7, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Two Rust photo apps on the Mac</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          The same test as <a
          href="/blogs/two-rust-photo-apps-in-the-browser.html">last
          time</a>, on the desktop. On the Mac, LightPhotos lets Apple decode
          the photos.
          <a href="https://github.com/storytold/lightcraft" target="_blank">LightCraft</a>
          decodes every photo with its own Rust code.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 7 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          I used the same 20 Sony RAW files in a full-screen window on a 4K
          display. LightPhotos filled its grid in 0.85&nbsp;s, and LightCraft
          in 1.37&nbsp;s. LightPhotos used 1.2&nbsp;GB of memory, and
          LightCraft used 1.4&nbsp;GB. The next photo was a tie: 0.55&nbsp;s
          against 0.60&nbsp;s. Both times are at the lowest value that my
          script can measure.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>The main difference: who decodes the pixels</h2>
      <p>
        On the desktop, both apps are usual Rust programs. Both use all CPU
        cores, draw with <code>wgpu</code> on Metal and read your folders in
        place. The important difference is where the pixels come from.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="decode-demo">
        <div class="lp-try-controls" role="group" aria-label="App">
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-mode="lp" aria-pressed="true">LightPhotos</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-mode="lc" aria-pressed="false">LightCraft</button>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 210" role="img" aria-label="Diagram of the decode path. LightPhotos sends a RAW file to Apple's ImageIO, which is part of macOS. LightCraft sends it to its own Rust decoders, which are inside the app. Both paths end on the screen.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WHERE THE PIXELS COME FROM</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="86" width="110" height="44" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="55" y="113" text-anchor="middle">RAW file</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="530" y="86" width="110" height="44" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="585" y="113" text-anchor="middle">Screen</text>

          <g data-lane="lp">
            <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M110 100 L220 52 M420 52 L530 100"></path>
            <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="220" y="30" width="200" height="44" rx="6"></rect>
            <text class="lp-fig-t" x="320" y="50" text-anchor="middle">Apple ImageIO</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="320" y="66" text-anchor="middle">part of macOS</text>
            <circle class="lp-fig-bar" r="5" data-dot>
              <animateMotion dur="2.4s" repeatCount="indefinite" path="M110 100 L220 52 L420 52 L530 100"></animateMotion>
            </circle>
          </g>
          <g data-lane="lc" opacity="0.35">
            <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M110 116 L220 164 M420 164 L530 116"></path>
            <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="220" y="142" width="200" height="44" rx="6"></rect>
            <text class="lp-fig-t" x="320" y="162" text-anchor="middle">Own Rust decoders</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="320" y="178" text-anchor="middle">inside the app</text>
            <circle class="lp-fig-bar" r="5" data-dot display="none">
              <animateMotion dur="2.4s" repeatCount="indefinite" path="M110 116 L220 164 L420 164 L530 116"></animateMotion>
            </circle>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <p class="lp-try-readout" aria-live="polite">LightPhotos: Apple decodes every JPEG, HEIC and RAW file. The decoders add 0&nbsp;MB to the 15&nbsp;MB app.</p>
        <figcaption>
          Select an app. LightPhotos uses the decoders that Preview and Photos
          use (see <a href="/blogs/what-imageio-gives-you-free.html">What
          ImageIO gives you free</a>). The Mac build does not include the RAW
          library that the other builds use.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        LightCraft also has many more finished features. It is a full
        Lightroom-style editor, with masks, healing, lens
        corrections, presets, history, HDR and panorama merges, and export to
        six formats. LightPhotos has a smaller job: you go through a folder
        fast, keep the good photos and make quick edits. Remember this for
        each number below.
      </p>

      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col">LightCraft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Decoding</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Apple's ImageIO, and CoreImage's <code>CIRAWFilter</code> for full-size RAW</td>
              <td>Its own, in Rust, for every format</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">HEIC</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Yes</td>
              <td>Not yet</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Canon CR3</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Yes</td>
              <td>Embedded preview only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Your photos</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Folders, read in place. Each folder keeps its own part of the catalog.</td>
              <td>One central catalog. Files stay in place unless you copy them.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">License</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">MIT or Apache-2.0, with no copyleft libraries in the Mac app</td>
              <td>MIT or Apache-2.0</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>One catalog or one in each folder</h2>
      <p>
        LightCraft keeps one central library, as Lightroom does. Its catalog records
        every photo, so searches, albums and stacks can include all of them.
        Import can add a photo in place, or copy or move it into
        <code>Originals/YYYY/YYYY-MM-DD/</code>. <strong>Browse
        Folder…</strong> also records what it sees. It removes those records
        later if you did not change the photos.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightPhotos does it differently. The app can open many folders, and
        each folder keeps its own small cache and index in the
        <code>.lightphotos</code> folder inside it. The catalog for a folder
        moves with the folder. You can move, copy, rename or delete a folder
        in Finder, and the app does not need to know, other than opening it again.
      </p>

      <h2>How I measured</h2>
      <p>
        I built both apps with their own release scripts. I ran them on one
        M4 Pro Mac with 48&nbsp;GB of memory and macOS 26.3. For each run, I
        copied the same 20 Sony A7&nbsp;II RAW files to a new folder, so no
        cache had seen them. Each app started with a new home folder and
        default settings.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each window was 3840 × 2000&nbsp;px on a 4K display. In a smaller
        window, the camera's embedded JPEG is large enough. Then neither app
        must develop the RAW data.
      </p>
      <p>
        A script, <code>tools/desktop-bench</code>, starts the app,
        double-clicks the first photo and pushes the right arrow eight times.
        It captures the window every 150&nbsp;ms. It records the first change,
        and the time when nothing changed for 3&nbsp;s. Each number is the
        median of five runs. One capture takes about 0.34&nbsp;s, so no
        result can be less than about 0.5&nbsp;s.
      </p>

      <h2>The numbers</h2>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick" style="width: 30%;">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col" style="width: 30%;">LightCraft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Download (.dmg)</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">7.4&nbsp;MB</td>
              <td>15.2&nbsp;MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">App, installed</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">15&nbsp;MB</td>
              <td>43&nbsp;MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Launch to window</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.27&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>0.43&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Launch to finished grid, 20 RAWs</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.85&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>1.37&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Open the first photo: first change / screen settled</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.57 / 1.46&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>0.67 / 0.67&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Next photo, each (median of 8): first change / screen settled</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.55 / 0.55&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>0.60 / 0.60&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Memory afterwards</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">1.2&nbsp;GB</td>
              <td>1.4&nbsp;GB</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The runs agreed to within a few percent, with three exceptions. The
        LightPhotos first photo took 1.0 to 1.5&nbsp;s. One LightCraft grid
        took 2.3&nbsp;s. LightCraft's memory was 1.3 to 1.7&nbsp;GB.
      </p>

      <TimingComparison
        id="mac-timing-demo"
        title="What the measurement sees"
        scale={2}
        milestones={["First visible change", "Whole screen settled"]}
        scenarios={macTimings}
        note="Fixed 0–2 s scale. Medians of five runs, full screen on a 4K display. The capture method cannot measure less than about 0.5 s, so the next-photo difference is too small to see. These times are for the whole window, not for a sharp photo. Do not compare them with the browser test."
      />

      <h2>Why the next photo is fast</h2>
      <p>
        Both apps prepare the photos on each side of the current photo. When
        you push the arrow key, the next photo is already developed. The app
        only has to show it.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="prefetch-demo">
        <div class="lp-try-controls">
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-next>Next photo →</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-reset>Start again</button>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 128" role="img" aria-label="A strip of seven photos. The open photo has an outline. The photos on each side of it are developed in the background, so they are ready before you move to them.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">PHOTOS IN THE FOLDER</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" data-onscreen x="62" y="32" text-anchor="middle">on screen</text>
          <g data-frames></g>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="24" y="108" width="12" height="10" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="42" y="117">Ready</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="100" y="108" width="12" height="10" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="118" y="117">Developing</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="200" y="108" width="12" height="10" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="218" y="117">Not prepared</text>
        </svg>
        <p class="lp-try-readout" aria-live="polite">Photo 1 is open. Nothing prepared it, so the app develops it now.</p>
        <figcaption>
          Push <strong>Next photo</strong> slowly, then push it fast. If you
          move faster than a develop, you must wait. Develop time is
          shown as 0.6&nbsp;s, about the time of one RAW develop at this
          window size.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        LightPhotos develops the neighbours with ImageIO, at the window size,
        up to 4096&nbsp;px. LightCraft uses its own code, up to 2560&nbsp;px.
        Both times are at the limit of the measurement, so I call them equal.
        Both apps pay the same cost: CPU time and battery for photos that you
        possibly do not look at.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing prepares the first photo. At this window size, the embedded
        JPEG (1616&nbsp;px wide) is too small. LightPhotos shows the grid
        thumbnail first, then develops the RAW data in about 0.6&nbsp;s.
        LightCraft shows the first photo in one step.
      </p>
      <p>
        My script waits a few seconds on each photo, as a person does who
        looks at each one. If you hold the arrow key down, you can go faster
        than either app can prepare. I did not measure that.
      </p>
      <p>
        For 100% zoom and export, LightPhotos uses CoreImage's
        <code>CIRAWFilter</code>. It uses the same Apple RAW engine, so the
        pixels agree to within rounding. But it runs on the GPU and
        demosaics only at the size that you ask for. A full 24-megapixel
        decode took 185&nbsp;ms, against 590&nbsp;ms through ImageIO. (Median
        of the same 20 files, one at a time.)
      </p>

      <h2>Why the grid is faster and memory is lower</h2>
      <p>
        For a grid thumbnail, LightPhotos asks ImageIO for a small image.
        ImageIO reads the embedded JPEG in 12 to 22&nbsp;ms per file.
        LightCraft also shows the embedded JPEGs first. Then it replaces them
        with thumbnails that it develops from the RAW data. Its grid is not
        finished until those thumbnails arrive.
      </p>
      <p>
        For memory, each app keeps three photos ready: the current photo and
        its two neighbours. But the two apps keep different pixels.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="memory-demo">
        <div class="lp-try-controls" role="group" aria-label="App">
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-mode="lc" aria-pressed="true">LightCraft</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-mode="lp" aria-pressed="false">LightPhotos</button>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 224" role="img" aria-label="Memory for one photo, drawn as a rectangle. The width is the number of pixels and the height is the bytes for each pixel. LightCraft keeps 4.4 million pixels at 12 bytes, about 52 MB. LightPhotos keeps up to 11.2 million pixels at 4 bytes, about 45 MB. The two areas are almost the same.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">MEMORY FOR ONE PHOTO = PIXELS × BYTES PER PIXEL</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" data-ghost x="96" y="140" width="400" height="50" stroke-dasharray="4 4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" data-area x="96" y="40" width="156" height="150" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-on-bar" data-mb x="174" y="120" text-anchor="middle">52 MB</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="96" y1="196" x2="560" y2="196"></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" data-wlabel x="96" y="214">4.4 million pixels (2560 × 1707)</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" data-hlabel x="88" y="119" text-anchor="end">12 bytes</text>
        </svg>
        <p class="lp-try-readout" aria-live="polite">LightCraft: 32-bit floating-point RGB. 2560 × 1707 × 12 bytes ≈ 52 MB a photo, 157 MB for three.</p>
        <figcaption>
          Select an app. The dashed outline is the other app. LightCraft keeps
          fewer pixels with more data in each. LightPhotos keeps more pixels
          with less data in each. Sizes are for a 3:2 photo at each app's
          largest size.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        With 12 bytes for each pixel, LightCraft's sliders can use the full
        range of the sensor: highlights above white, and exposure changes
        without banding. LightPhotos gets finished pixels from ImageIO.
        The totals are almost the same, and on screen the photos look the
        same.
      </p>
      <p>
        The app size also comes from ImageIO. LightCraft includes its own
        decoders for RAW, JPEG, JPEG XL and more, and its own colour
        management. Its release build also skips link-time optimisation and
        keeps some debug information. The macOS decoders add nothing to
        LightPhotos.
      </p>

      <h2>What each choice costs</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>LightPhotos looks as Apple develops a photo.</strong> I
          calibrated LightPhotos on Linux, Windows and the browser to match
          ImageIO, so a photo looks very similar on each platform.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>LightPhotos on the Mac supports only the cameras that
          Apple supports.</strong> A new camera works when Apple adds it. If ImageIO
          decodes a file incorrectly, I cannot fix it.
          <code>CIRAWFilter</code> uses the same engine, so it does not change
          this. LightPhotos on other platforms uses rawler, so it supports
          every camera that rawler supports.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>LightCraft has one decoder everywhere.</strong> A photo
          looks the same on each platform, and LightCraft can fix any bug in
          its decoder. The cost is a larger app, and formats that it does not
          have yet. HEIC needs an HEVC decoder, and no pure-Rust one with a
          permissive license exists. CR3 files show only their embedded
          preview for now.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Wrapping up</h2>
      <p>
        On the Mac, LightPhotos relies on the operating system, and LightCraft
        relies on itself. LightPhotos is smaller, starts faster, fills its
        grid faster and uses a little less memory. Apple did that work, and a
        smaller feature set fits in a smaller app. Both apps prepare the
        photos on each side, so both are equally fast through a shoot.
        LightCraft looks the same everywhere and does much more when you
        start to edit.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/two-rust-photo-apps-in-the-browser.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Two Rust photo apps in the browser</span>
        </a>
        <a class="lp-article-nav-next" href="/blogs/linux-raw-photo-editor.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Next</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">A fast RAW photo editor for Linux</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>

  <script>
    const reduce = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;

    // Wire a row of [data-mode] toggle buttons to one render function.
    function toggle(fig, render) {
      const btns = fig.querySelectorAll("[data-mode]");
      btns.forEach((b) => b.addEventListener("click", () => {
        btns.forEach((o) => o.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(o === b)));
        render(b.dataset.mode);
      }));
    }

    // Decode path. Facts from the post: 15 MB and 43 MB installed.
    const decode = document.getElementById("decode-demo");
    if (decode) {
      const readout = decode.querySelector(".lp-try-readout");
      const text = {
        lp: "LightPhotos: Apple decodes every JPEG, HEIC and RAW file. The decoders add 0 MB to the 15 MB app.",
        lc: "LightCraft: its own Rust code decodes and demosaics every file. The decoders are inside the 43 MB app.",
      };
      toggle(decode, (mode) => {
        decode.querySelectorAll("[data-lane]").forEach((g) => {
          const on = g.dataset.lane === mode;
          g.setAttribute("opacity", on ? "1" : "0.35");
          g.querySelector("rect").setAttribute("class", on ? "lp-fig-box-accent" : "lp-fig-box");
          g.querySelector("[data-dot]").setAttribute("display", on && !reduce ? "inline" : "none");
        });
        readout.textContent = text[mode];
      });
      if (reduce) decode.querySelectorAll("[data-dot]").forEach((d) => d.setAttribute("display", "none"));
    }

    // Prefetch strip. Each photo the app wants (the open photo and its two
    // neighbours) develops in DEVELOP ms; photos outside that window are dropped.
    const prefetch = document.getElementById("prefetch-demo");
    if (prefetch) {
      const N = 7, DEVELOP = 600, W = 76, GAP = 14, X0 = 24, Y = 44, H = 52;
      const NS = "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg";
      const host = prefetch.querySelector("[data-frames]");
      const onscreen = prefetch.querySelector("[data-onscreen]");
      const readout = prefetch.querySelector(".lp-try-readout");
      const el = (tag, attrs) => {
        const node = document.createElementNS(NS, tag);
        for (const k in attrs) node.setAttribute(k, attrs[k]);
        return node;
      };
      const frames = Array.from({ length: N }, (_, i) => {
        const x = X0 + i * (W + GAP);
        const g = el("g", {});
        const box = el("rect", { class: "lp-fig-box", x, y: Y, width: W, height: H, rx: 4 });
        const fill = el("rect", { class: "lp-fig-bar-dim", x, y: Y, width: 0, height: H, rx: 4 });
        const label = el("text", { class: "lp-fig-t", x: x + W / 2, y: Y + 31, "text-anchor": "middle" });
        const ring = el("rect", { x: x - 3, y: Y - 3, width: W + 6, height: H + 6, rx: 6, fill: "none", stroke: "var(--lp-ink)", "stroke-width": 2 });
        label.textContent = String(i + 1);
        g.append(box, fill, label, ring);
        host.append(g);
        return { x, fill, label, ring, progress: 0 };
      });
      let current = 0, last = 0, waiting = false;

      const wanted = (i) => Math.abs(i - current) <= 1;
      function paint() {
        frames.forEach((f, i) => {
          const ready = f.progress >= 1;
          f.fill.setAttribute("width", W * Math.min(1, f.progress));
          f.fill.setAttribute("class", ready ? "lp-fig-bar" : "lp-fig-bar-dim");
          f.label.setAttribute("class", ready ? "lp-fig-on-bar" : "lp-fig-t");
          f.ring.setAttribute("display", i === current ? "inline" : "none");
        });
        onscreen.setAttribute("x", frames[current].x + W / 2);
      }
      function tick(now) {
        const dt = Math.max(0, now - last);
        last = now;
        frames.forEach((f, i) => { if (wanted(i) && f.progress < 1) f.progress = Math.min(1, f.progress + dt / DEVELOP); });
        if (waiting && frames[current].progress >= 1) {
          waiting = false;
          readout.textContent = `Photo ${current + 1} is on screen. The app now prepares its neighbours.`;
        }
        paint();
        if (frames.some((f, i) => wanted(i) && f.progress < 1)) requestAnimationFrame(tick);
      }
      function start() {
        last = performance.now();
        requestAnimationFrame(tick);
      }
      function open(i, fresh) {
        current = i;
        frames.forEach((f, j) => { if (!wanted(j)) f.progress = 0; });
        const ready = frames[i].progress >= 1;
        waiting = !ready;
        readout.textContent = fresh
          ? "Photo 1 is open. Nothing prepared it, so the app develops it now."
          : ready
            ? `Photo ${i + 1} was ready. It shows at once.`
            : `Photo ${i + 1} is not ready yet. You wait while the app develops it.`;
        paint();
        start();
      }
      prefetch.querySelector("[data-next]").addEventListener("click", () => {
        if (current < N - 1) open(current + 1, false);
        else readout.textContent = "This is the last photo. Push Start again.";
      });
      prefetch.querySelector("[data-reset]").addEventListener("click", () => {
        frames.forEach((f) => { f.progress = 0; });
        open(0, true);
      });
      open(0, true);
    }

    // Memory area. Width is megapixels, height is bytes per pixel, so the
    // area is the memory for one photo. 3:2 photos at each app's largest size.
    const memory = document.getElementById("memory-demo");
    if (memory) {
      const X = 96, BASE = 190, PX_PER_MP = 400 / 11.19, PX_PER_BYTE = 150 / 12;
      const apps = {
        lc: { mp: 4.37, bytes: 12, mb: 52, dims: "2560 × 1707", name: "LightCraft: 32-bit floating-point RGB", total: 157 },
        lp: { mp: 11.19, bytes: 4, mb: 45, dims: "4096 × 2731", name: "LightPhotos: finished RGBA", total: 134 },
      };
      const area = memory.querySelector("[data-area]");
      const ghost = memory.querySelector("[data-ghost]");
      const mb = memory.querySelector("[data-mb]");
      const wlabel = memory.querySelector("[data-wlabel]");
      const hlabel = memory.querySelector("[data-hlabel]");
      const readout = memory.querySelector(".lp-try-readout");
      const size = (a) => [a.mp * PX_PER_MP, a.bytes * PX_PER_BYTE];
      let shown = size(apps.lc), frame = 0;

      function draw([w, h]) {
        area.setAttribute("width", w);
        area.setAttribute("height", h);
        area.setAttribute("y", BASE - h);
        mb.setAttribute("x", X + w / 2);
        mb.setAttribute("y", BASE - h / 2 + 4);
        hlabel.setAttribute("y", BASE - h / 2 + 4);
        shown = [w, h];
      }
      toggle(memory, (mode) => {
        const a = apps[mode], other = apps[mode === "lc" ? "lp" : "lc"];
        const [gw, gh] = size(other);
        ghost.setAttribute("width", gw);
        ghost.setAttribute("height", gh);
        ghost.setAttribute("y", BASE - gh);
        mb.textContent = `${a.mb} MB`;
        hlabel.textContent = `${a.bytes} bytes`;
        wlabel.textContent = `${a.mp.toFixed(1)} million pixels (${a.dims})`;
        readout.textContent = `${a.name}. ${a.dims} × ${a.bytes} bytes ≈ ${a.mb} MB a photo, ${a.total} MB for three.`;
        const from = shown, to = size(a);
        cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
        if (reduce) return draw(to);
        const t0 = performance.now();
        const step = (now) => {
          const t = Math.min(1, (now - t0) / 500);
          const e = t < 0.5 ? 2 * t * t : 1 - (-2 * t + 2) ** 2 / 2;
          draw([from[0] + (to[0] - from[0]) * e, from[1] + (to[1] - from[1]) * e]);
          if (t < 1) frame = requestAnimationFrame(step);
        };
        frame = requestAnimationFrame(step);
      });
    }
  </script>
</Base>
